# Markdown hard line breaks are encoded as TWO TRAILING SPACES: without them the renderer
# joins a stacked metadata block (**Version:** / **Date:** / **Status:** / **Target:**) into
# one run-on line. `.editorconfig` already stops editors trimming them ([*.md]
# trim_trailing_whitespace = false); this stops git from doing the same:
#
#   * `git diff --check` no longer reports every field line as an error.
#   * `git apply --whitespace=fix` (and `git am`) no longer SILENTLY STRIP them, which is
#     verified behaviour, not a precaution.
#
# Scoped to the one check that matters — space-before-tab and friends stay active on .md, and
# every other file type is untouched. Deliberately NO text/eol attribute here: adding one would
# rewrite line endings across the whole worktree.
#
# Convention + rationale: .agents/skills/create-design-doc/SKILL.md (Step 3).
# Verify with: python Tools/Python/check_markdown_breaks.py
*.md whitespace=-blank-at-eol

# NOTE ON SYNTAX (verified 2026-08-07, the hard way): every line is `pattern attr...` with
# exactly ONE pattern. Grouping patterns — `*.png *.jpg binary` — does not merely fail for the
# later patterns, it discards the WHOLE line: git rejects `*.jpg` as an invalid attribute name,
# so even `*.png` comes back `binary: unspecified`. Silent no-op. Keep one pattern per line.

# --- Line endings ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The index already stores LF throughout (core.autocrlf=true normalized on every commit), so
# this changes only what git writes at CHECKOUT. It makes the worktree consistent across clones
# regardless of each machine's core.autocrlf, and matches both .editorconfig (end_of_line = lf)
# and what Unity itself writes for YAML/.meta.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING (verified 2026-08-07): this block must stay ABOVE the binary section.
# The last matching line wins PER ATTRIBUTE, so putting `* text=auto` after `*.png binary`
# overrides the macro's `-text` and leaves binaries at `text: auto` — back to content sniffing,
# the exact misdetection the binary net exists to prevent. Above it, binaries keep `text: unset`.
#
# `text=auto` CAN SILENTLY SKIP A FILE: a single lone CR (a CR not part of a CRLF) makes git's
# auto-detection classify the content as binary, after which it normalizes nothing. That is not
# hypothetical — GenerationJobData.cs ended `}<CR>` with no trailing newline and was the sole
# CRLF survivor of the 2026-08-07 conversion, reported by git as `i/-text w/-text` while every
# other file converted. An explicit `diff=` driver still forces a readable diff for such a file,
# so the only visible symptom is that its line endings never change. Audit with
# `git ls-files --eol` and look for `-text` on anything that is not genuinely binary.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one format that genuinely needs CRLF. None exist today; this is a
# guard for future additions. Below the `*` line so the more specific rule wins.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# --- Diff hunk headers (built-in drivers; verified 2026-08-05) -------------------
*.cs    diff=csharp
*.md    diff=markdown

# --- Binary safety net ----------------------------------------------------------
# Git guesses binary from NUL bytes in the first 8 KB; a file that looks textual can be
# misdetected and EOL-mangled. `binary` == `-diff -merge -text`.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.tga           binary
*.psd           binary
*.tif           binary
*.tiff          binary
*.exr           binary
*.hdr           binary
*.fbx           binary
*.blend         binary
*.obj           binary
*.dae           binary
*.3ds           binary
*.wav           binary
*.mp3           binary
*.ogg           binary
*.aiff          binary
*.aif           binary
*.ttf           binary
*.ttc           binary
*.otf           binary
*.dll           binary
*.so            binary
*.dylib         binary
*.a             binary
*.bundle        binary
*.pdb           binary
*.mdb           binary
*.unitypackage  binary
*.pdf           binary
*.cubemap       binary

# --- Unity YAML smart merge -----------------------------------------------------
# Safe to declare before configuring the driver: an UNDEFINED merge driver falls back to git's
# default text merge with normal conflict markers (verified in a sandbox repo). Wiring
# merge.unityyamlmerge.driver to Unity's UnityYAMLMerge.exe is a separate, per-machine step.
#
# These are YAML, NOT binary — asset serialization is Force Text (m_SerializationMode: 2).
# NEVER give *.unity/*.prefab/*.asset/*.meta the `binary` or `-text` attribute: it destroys
# their diffs and makes scene/prefab merge conflicts unresolvable. That is the real corruption
# risk with these types — not their mere presence in this file.
*.unity           merge=unityyamlmerge
*.prefab          merge=unityyamlmerge
*.asset           merge=unityyamlmerge
*.mat             merge=unityyamlmerge
*.anim            merge=unityyamlmerge
*.controller      merge=unityyamlmerge
*.physicMaterial  merge=unityyamlmerge

# --- Git LFS: deliberately absent — do NOT add filter=lfs entries here ----------
# LFS was installed on this repo once and scrubbed on 2026-08-07. It never tracked a single
# file: `git lfs ls-files` was empty, and no pointer was ever committed on any branch
# (`git log --all -S 'version https://git-lfs.github.com'` and `git grep` in HEAD both empty).
# The residue was purely local — 4 hooks, 2 `lfs.*` keys in .git/config, and 60 KB of lock
# caches holding ZERO objects — so `git lfs uninstall --local` removed all of it. No
# `git lfs migrate` and no history rewrite were needed then, and none would be needed now.
#
# The 4 `filter.lfs.*` keys in the machine's GLOBAL ~/.gitconfig were deliberately LEFT IN
# PLACE: they are machine-wide and may serve other repositories. They are inert here precisely
# because this file declares no `filter=lfs` pattern — adding one is what would wake them up.
#
# If a large-asset problem appears, solve it some other way. Re-adding LFS re-creates the
# pre-push hook that aborts every push whenever git-lfs is off PATH, which is what it cost
# last time.
#
# Verification trap: ANY `git lfs ...` command — including `git lfs ls-files`, the natural way
# to check the scrub held — rewrites `lfs.repositoryformatversion=0` into .git/config as a side
# effect. Seeing that key again does NOT mean LFS came back; it means someone ran a `git lfs`
# command. It is inert on its own, and hooks and .git/lfs/ are NOT recreated. Check with
# `git config --local --get-regexp '^lfs\.'` instead, which does not perturb what it measures.
